{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_III,_Count_of_Tripoli\" without commas and with at least 3 highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Antioch and ruled the County of Tripoli from 1178 until his death in 1186\nHe was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena of Armenia\nRaymond III became count at a young age after his father was murdered in 1178\n*Early Life and Reign* \nRaymond III was initially under the regency of Bohemond of Chastellet but soon took control of the county\nHe played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a key figure in the conflict between Hugh III of Le Puiset and Guy of Lusignan\nRaymond III supported Hugh III and was opposed to the rule of Guy of Lusignan\nIn 1183 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Saladin during a raid but was released after paying a ransom\n*Capture and Release by Saladin*\nRaymond III then formed an alliance with Saladin in an effort to counter the power of Guy of Lusignan\nThis alliance was short lived as Raymond III soon rejoined the Kingdom of Jerusalem in preparation for the Battle of Hattin\n*Alliance with Saladin and Battle of Hattin*\nThe Battle of Hattin was a decisive defeat for the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Raymond III was one of the few leaders to escape\nRaymond III died in 1186 while besieging the castle of Safed which was held by Saladin forces\nHis death led to the county of Tripoli being inherited by his cousin Bohemond IV of Antioch\nRaymond III is often portrayed as a wise and capable leader who was faced with difficult circumstances during his reign\nDespite his efforts the county of Tripoli was eventually conquered by Saladin in 1187\nThe legacy of Raymond III has been the subject of much debate among historians with s